# Purpose and audience: executable specification evidence for the owning engineering team.
# @req REQ-SSPEC-LIB
# research: doc/01_research/domain/sspec_documentization_maintenance.md ; plan: doc/03_plan/sspec_modernization_plan.md ; architecture: doc/04_architecture/sspec_documentization_maintenance.md ; design: doc/05_design/infra/sspec/modern_sspec_typed_evidence_design.md

"""
Regression guard for STOMP `content-length` parsing.

History: this guard used to require `return value.to_int() ?? nil`. That
pattern is DEAD -- `.to_int()` is typed `i64?` but its runtime cannot produce
nil, so the `??` arm never fired and `content-length:abc` read as 0.
`subscribe.spl` returns `try_parse_int(value)`, which really can report
failure, and a non-numeric header is treated as the protocol error it is.
"""

use std.spec.step

use std.convert.{try_parse_int}

describe "nogc sync stomp content length numeric guard":
    describe("numeric header values parse to their integer value"):
        it "parses numeric content-length values":
            # @req REQ-SSPEC-LIB
            expect(try_parse_int("128")).to_equal(128)  # oracle: 128 is the literal header value
            expect(try_parse_int("0")).to_equal(0)  # oracle: zero is a valid length

    describe("non-numeric headers must be a reportable failure, never 0"):
        it "reports failure for non-numeric content-length":
            # @req REQ-SSPEC-LIB
            expect(try_parse_int("abc") == nil).to_equal(true)
            expect(try_parse_int("") == nil).to_equal(true)
